<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tutankhamun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1342–1323 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a> · Also known as Tutankhamen, Tutankhaten, King Tut · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tutankhamun was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty</a> of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> who ruled c. 1332–1323 BCE and died at about the age of 19. Born Tutankhaten in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>, he restored the traditional religion after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atenism">Atenism</a>, restoring the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, changing his name and moving the court from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> back to Memphis. DNA testing identified his father as the mummy from tomb KV55, thought to be Akhenaten or Smenkhkare. As his royal tomb was unfinished, he was buried in a small non-royal tomb, which excavators led by Howard Carter found in 1922 with much of its contents, over 5,000 artifacts and the king's undisturbed mummy; the discovery renewed public interest in ancient Egypt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tutankhamun.jpg" alt="Tutankhamun" width="330" height="465" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MykReeve · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tutanchamun_Maske.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…as Egypt declined: the Akkar Plain rebelled with Hittite support to become the kingdom of Amurru, the Mitanni Empire that had dominated Syria fell apart under the campaigns of Suppiluliuma I of Hatti, and <b>Tutankhamun</b> and his general Horemheb scrambled to keep Egypt's hold on the southern Levant.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death, under the young <b>Tutankhamun</b>, the old gods were restored, and later kings erased Akhenaten from the record.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>At the British Museum, “The First Emperor: China’s Terracotta Army” ran from 2007 to 2008, brought the most visitors since the <b>Tutankhamun</b> exhibition of 1972, and sold 400,000 advance tickets so fast that the museum extended its opening hours until midnight.</blockquote><a href="/work/terracotta-army">Terracotta Army · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>War was the quickest way up for an able man: soldiers won rewards and land, and Horemheb, a general under <b>Tutankhamun</b>, became king himself.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>His reforms were later regarded as heretical and reversed under his successor <b>Tutankhamun</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/great-hymn-to-the-aten">Great Hymn to the Aten</a></li><li><blockquote>With Lord Carnarvon present, Howard Carter's team cleared the stairway and found a sealed doorway bearing <b>Tutankhamun</b>'s name.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-24">24 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
